lly abused his girlfriend  that he had illegal weapons the  complaints  went nowhere the rcmp's failures before  during and after the massacre have  shaken people's trust  criminologist daryl davies says rcmp  clearly have not learned from  previous deadly shooting rampages like  moncton new brunswick in 2014.  port-a-pick is more of a i would say a  gross exaggeration  of you know of the organization's  inability to be able to handle that type  of situation  witnesses have told police wartman had  become increasingly agitated about the  pandemic  davies says rcmp should have been better  prepared  in light of the lockdown and the  pandemic and everything the possibility  that some  it would trigger something like this in  somebody who was you know mentally  unstable  was a very high probability they got the  killer right here  when the onslaught finally ended it was  by chance an rcmp officer who stopped  for fuel at this gas station  encountered wartman who had stolen a car  from one of his victims  and needed gas  the officer shot and killed the gunman  at 11 26 a.m on april 19th  13 hours after the massacre started  in all he killed 22 people at 16 crime  scenes  including rcmp veteran constable heidi  stevenson  a mother of two children  nearly eight months later families and  entire communities are struggling to  move forward  we're trying to figure out what who who  are we now  serena lewis is nova scotia's grief  coordinator  she lives near puerto pick her own  family was locked down during the  massacre  we can't go back to before april 18th  that that doesn't exist anymore  so we will shape a new identity some  people globally have told me this could  be a 20-year span for these communities  that's if we lean in lewis says grief  counseling is already under resourced in  nova scotia  and healing is even harder during the  pandemic when physical contact is  discouraged  now common things can be emotional  triggers  even the mere sight of an rcmp vehicle  you know if you see a car coming with  the lights on behind you  what does that do for people i was in a  a gathering uh the other day and i had  to stop everybody because i could feel  it and i said you know kind of how's  everybody doing  here tonight given the fact that we've  had the largest mass shooting  in canada and a woman looked at me and  she said are we really allowed to talk  about that elephant  for families pursuing answers has led to  more pain  on december 4 the rcmp did announce it  was charging wartman's common law  partner lisa banfield and two others  with illegally transferring ammunition  to the gunman  rcmp claimed the three had no prior  knowledge of ortman's actions on april  18th and 19th  but many other questions remain  grief-stricken families had to protest  before the federal and nova scotia  governments  agreed to a full public inquiry they'll  have to wait  two years for the inquiry's final report  the massacre has drawn condolences from  across the country  but the outpouring only goes so far  victim's relatives like jenny kirsten  are struggling to cope with their grief  it was easier when there was so much  adrenaline  and now it's sinking in  before his dad was killed tyler blair  and his partner had a baby daughter  he remembers how happy his dad was when  he found out he'd be a grandfather  i told my dad on her way to work one  morning his face just lit up  he was no he was super excited  he was more excited than i was i think  she will only know her grandparents from  pictures i'll make sure  she knows how much they loved her  some residents are literally moving on  port-a-pick is dotted with for sale  signs  leon jodry has not slept in his house  since that horrific night  he's decided to leave hoping that will  finally  bring him peace i can't sleep here can't  shut my eyes so  it's my choice not to stay here i'm  selling mine i can't speak for anybody  else i'm selling mine for the images i  got in my head of driving around that  night i drive here it's all i see  next week on the new reality things have  really accelerated with wave 2  and it's disheartening there's certainly  there's no other way to put it  our staff are fatigued so half the icu  right now is  they're all on life support in one way  or the other we're seeing outbreaks and  families and we're seeing that when  family members are visiting other family  members and putting those people at risk  how worried are you about going into  christmas and what might be waiting for  us on the other side of that  very like unequivocally very  that is the new reality for this week  i'm donna friesen  thanks for watching  you
 

